cartridge "SPACE WORMS/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1989"

' SPACE WORMS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Space Worms", COMPUTE!'s Gazette April 1989
' disk (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS 2064: a packed program that
' unpacks itself to $5000-$78FF). No author is named in the program or on
' the disk menu; it is credited to the magazine. Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (played in jsc64
' and read from its machine code, orig/sworms-ml.dis), the game is built for
' the ColecoVision: rising stars, a dart ship, a six-segment worm of round
' wriggling sprites, sounds, a title tune.
'
' The game: a space worm of six segments loops across the sky. Your ship
' moves in a band at the bottom of the screen and fires one shot at a time
' straight up. Every shot that hits the worm scores 1; every 8 hits the
' worm loses its tail segment (10 points, except the first). When the head
' goes the level is cleared: 100 points, and a new worm comes on a new path.
' Touching the worm costs a ship. Five ships (LIVES:4 plus the one in play).
'
' From the original (orig/): the worm's six segments follow one of eight X
' loops and one of eight Y loops (C64 tables at $5000-$5865, kept as
' numbers), each segment one step a frame behind the one before (level 1:
' X 3 steps apart, Y 3 steps apart with the X phase 28 steps ahead; levels
' 2-4: 3, 5-9: 4, 10-19: 6, 20 on: 7 steps apart, so the worm stretches);
' each new level picks an X loop and a Y loop other than the last ones, a
' head (3 kinds), bodies (3 kinds, all alike or mixed) and colours (one of
' three palettes or one random colour), and maybe a colour ripple along the
' worm; the ship moves 2 C64 pixels a frame inside X 32-312, Y 185-227;
' button held = a shot whenever none is in the air, 5 pixels a frame up;
' explosion 54 frames, 75 more, then the next ship at the far left or far
' right; the worm keeps moving all the while; 40 frames after a clear the
' next worm; stars rise at 1-7 pixels a frame (16 of them).
'
' Liberties: the field is scaled (C64 X 32..312 -> 16..224, Y by 0.88) and
' hits are box tests on the drawn bodies (the C64 used its sprite collision
' register); a shot ends at the top of the field (C64 Y 60) under the score
' line, not at Y 25; the level counter stops at 99. The colour ripple moves
' every 20 frames (C64: 4) and the GAME OVER screen does not flash (house
' rule: nothing faster than 3 Hz); the ship's flame flickers every 8
' frames (C64: 4).
' Added, so the game can't be won by parking (on the C64 level 1's loop
' never comes down to the ship's band, and the far left corner is out of
' every loop's reach): SPORES. Every 230 frames (10 fewer a level, at least
' 90) the head, when it is above the ship, drops a spore aimed at where the
' ship is, falling 2 C64 pixels a frame (3 from level 6, 4 from level 12).
' A spore kills like the worm; a shot destroys it (no points). A ship that
' keeps moving dodges every spore. Also added: a title page, a credits page,
' sound effects (the original was silent), a title tune.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the ship, either button
' fires (hold it for a stream of single shots). On the title, up and down
' choose, a button chooses. (START / HOW TO PLAY / CREDITS)
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

